    <h3>1970s: The Era of Oil Shocks</h3>
    <p>
      The decade opened with prices below $2/bbl—a legacy of the postwar pricing regime controlled by multinational oil companies. The 1973 OPEC embargo tripled prices to over $12, and the Iranian Revolution (1979) pushed them past $40. Average price for the decade: <strong>${decade_stats["1970s"]["mean"]:.2f}/bbl</strong>, with a range of ${decade_stats["1970s"]["range"]:.2f}. This decade's volatility fundamentally restructured global energy economics.
    </p>

    <h3>1980s: Counter-Shock and Decline</h3>
    <p>
      Prices peaked early in the decade near $40 then entered a secular decline, culminating in the 1986 price collapse when Saudi Arabia abandoned its swing-producer role and flooded the market. Prices fell from the mid-$20s to below $10 within months. Average: <strong>${decade_stats["1980s"]["mean"]:.2f}/bbl</strong>. The decade demonstrated that supply discipline within OPEC was fragile.
    </p>

    <h3>1990s: Range-Bound Calm</h3>
    <p>
      The 1990s were comparatively tranquil, with prices oscillating between ${decade_stats["1990s"]["min"]:.2f} and ${decade_stats["1990s"]["max"]:.2f} and averaging <strong>${decade_stats["1990s"]["mean"]:.2f}/bbl</strong>. The Gulf War spike (August 1990) briefly pushed prices above $34, but the disruption was short-lived. The Asian financial crisis (1997–98) drove prices to decade lows near $11.
    </p>

    <h3>2000s: The Super-Cycle</h3>
    <p>
      Driven by surging Chinese demand, tight spare capacity, and financial speculation, prices staged an unprecedented rally from the mid-$20s in 2000 to a peak of <strong>${max_price:.2f}</strong> in July 2008. The financial crisis then triggered the largest absolute collapse in history, with prices plunging to $41.34 by December 2008—a decline of nearly $92 in five months. Average: <strong>${decade_stats["2000s"]["mean"]:.2f}/bbl</strong>.
    </p>

    <h3>2010s: Shale Revolution and OPEC+ Rebalancing</h3>
    <p>
      The decade recorded the highest average price at <strong>${decade_stats["2010s"]["mean"]:.2f}/bbl</strong>. The early years saw prices above $100 sustained by supply constraints, but the U.S. shale boom shifted the landscape. By 2014–16, OPEC's decision to maintain production triggered a price war, crashing prices from $108 to below $30. The formation of OPEC+ in late 2016 began a gradual rebalancing. Std deviation of ${decade_stats["2010s"]["std"]:.2f} reflects this broad range.
    </p>

    <h3>2020s: Pandemic, War, and Transition (Partial Decade)</h3>
    <p>
      The COVID-19 demand shock drove prices to ${decade_stats["2020s"]["min"]:.2f}/bbl in April 2020—the lowest level since 1999. The recovery was equally dramatic, with the Russia-Ukraine conflict pushing prices back above $116 in June 2022. The partial decade averages <strong>${decade_stats["2020s"]["mean"]:.2f}/bbl</strong> with a range of ${decade_stats["2020s"]["range"]:.2f}, and the most recent observations show a gradual moderation toward the $60–$70 range.
